## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a beginner-friendly, real-world performance plan that isolates bottlenecks in a MongoDB-backed service without changing business logic.

## Key Concepts
- Ramp/test design in stages
- Synthetic data and non-prod data handling
- Endpoint, service, and DB bottleneck isolation
- Basic metrics: p95, p99, error rate, throughput, DB queue depth

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example k6 script sketch
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  http.get('https://svc.example/api/profile/123');
  sleep(0.5);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if p95 latency targets are missed?
- How would you validate results across multiple regions or environments?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical understanding of end-to-end latency, backpressure, and lightweight tooling for a common media pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency and tail latency
- Queue depth and backpressure
- Throughput ramping and baseline checks
- Lightweight tooling (k6) and basic metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import {sleep} from 'k6';

export let options = {vus: 2000, duration: '2m'};

export default function () {
  http.get('https://api.example.com/thumbnail?image_url=https://images.example.com/foo.jpg');
  sleep(0.1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate whether gateway, queue, or worker time dominates?
- How would you adapt tests if image processing time is the bottleneck?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess end-to-end latency across a fan-out path, backpressure resilience, region-aware autoscaling, and observability for a large-scale chat system with moderation integration.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end latency and tail behavior (P99/P99.9)
- Backpressure and queue depth management
- Region-aware autoscaling and load distribution
- Observability: tracing, metrics, dashboards, and alerting
- Fault injection: moderation outages, latency spikes, network partitions

## Code Example

```javascript
// P99 latency measurement sketch
function onMessageSent(event) {
  const t0 = performance.now();
  deliverToRecipients(event);
  const latency = performance.now() - t0;
  recordMetric('e2e_latency_ms', latency);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you model and test regional failover guarantees?
- What telemetry would you add to differentiate moderation latency from delivery latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time presence is sensitive to tail latency and backpressure. This question probes how an interviewer evaluates scalable WebSocket gateways, heartbeat resilience, and autoscaling behavior under bursty traffic, mirroring production issues at large-scale messaging apps.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket scale and connection churn
- Backpressure propagation across gateway and bus
- Heartbeat/timestamp accuracy and liveness tuning
- Autoscaling strategies (Kubernetes/HPA, event-driven scaling)
- Test harnesses: k6/ws, Gatling, Locust

## Code Example
```
import ws from 'k6/ws';
export const options = {vus: 1000, duration: '2m'};
export default function () {
  const url = 'wss://presence.example';
  ws.connect(url, {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('open', function () {
      socket.send(JSON.stringify({type:'presence', status:'online'}));
    });
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate latency components (gateway vs. downstream services)?
- How would you validate a canary rollout maintains SLAs under burst traffic?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical considerations for testing a real-time fraud pipeline under backpressure, ensuring end-to-end latency remains bounded while monitoring cascading effects across feature store, cache, and inference.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and saturation in microservices
- Tail latency and queue depth as bottleneck signals
- Circuit breakers and graceful degradation
- Observability: tracing, metrics, and dashboards
- Workload modeling with bursts and soak phases

## Code Example

```javascript
// k6 example for staged load on a fraud path
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export let options = { stages: [ { duration: '2m', target: 5000 }, { duration: '4m', target: 50000 }, { duration: '2m', target: 5000 } ] };
export default function () {
  http.post('https://fraud.example.com/ingest', JSON.stringify({ userId: 123, event: 'transaction', value: 42 }));
  sleep(0.01);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you differentiate cache-related vs GPU-inference bottlenecks in your measurements?
- What are your trigger thresholds for circuit breakers and when would you disable backoff to recover?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design a comprehensive performance test strategy for AI inference systems operating under multi-tenant workloads with GPU autoscaling and production rollout risks.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant isolation and quota enforcement
- GPU memory pressure handling and dynamic batch sizing
- Backpressure and queueing behavior under burst loads
- Rollback and feature-flag safety mechanisms during rollouts

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo workload generator sketch
const config = {
  tenants: 10,
  qps: 25000,
  batchSizes: [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32],
  duration: '30m',
  rampUp: '5m'
};

// Track latency percentiles
function trackLatency(tenant, latencyMs) {
  percentiles.tenant[tenant].push(latencyMs);
}

// Monitor GPU pressure
gpuMetrics.on('memoryPressure', (utilization) => {
  if (utilization > 0.9) triggerAlert();
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

To assess how candidates craft end-to-end performance tests for ultra-low-latency pipelines with multiple services and external dependencies, ensuring backpressure, fail-fast behavior, and autoscaling.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end and tail latency
- Bounded buffers, backpressure
- Circuit breakers and fail-fast
- Traffic shaping and ramp-up
- Observability and tracing (OpenTelemetry, Jaeger)
- Kubernetes autoscaling (HPA, KEDA optional)

## Code Example

```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export let options = { vus: 1000, duration: '60s' };
export default function () {
  const r = http.post('https://ads.example.com/bid', JSON.stringify({bid: 1}), { headers: { 'Content-Type':'application/json' }});
  check(r, { 'status 200': (r)=> r.status===200 });
  sleep(0.0005);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure and isolate downstream latency vs upstream latency in a live campaign?
- How would you simulate upstream outages without affecting other tenants in a shared cluster?

## Why This Is Asked
Testing for multi-tenant GPU contention and memory fragmentation—particularly after model changes—is critical for real-time inference systems in production environments. Understanding how to isolate GPU resources, detect performance degradation, and maintain SLA guarantees across tenants demonstrates deep infrastructure expertise.

## Key Concepts
- GPU resource isolation using MIG (Multi-Instance GPU) on NVIDIA hardware
- NVML metrics for granular GPU telemetry
- Triton Inference Server integration patterns
- Memory fragmentation versus transient spikes
- Kernel contention and cross-tenant interference detection
- Burst traffic modeling with per-tenant quotas
- Horizontal and vertical autoscaling strategies
- SLO validation and reliability signal collection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo workload generator for multi-tenant GPU testing
function simulateTenantLoad(tenants, ratePerTenant) {
  tenants.forEach(tenant => {
    const requests = generatePoissonRequests(ratePerTenant);
    requests.forEach(req => {
      const batchSize = getRandomBatchSize();
      const startTime = Date.now();
      executeInference(tenant, req, batchSize);
      recordMetrics(tenant, Date.now() - startTime);
    });
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a common pain point: cache vs external service bottlenecks in a two-path API, with realistic load and minimal code changes.

## Key Concepts
- k6 load testing across multiple variants
- Tail latency: p95/p99, error rate, throughput
- Cache hit ratio vs external latency and backpressure
- Data strategy and non-invasive isolation (mocking external services)

## Code Example
````javascript
import http from "k6/http";
import { check, sleep } from "k6";
export let options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: "5m", target: 8000 },
    { duration: "15m", target: 8000 }
  ]
};
export default function () {
  http.get("https://example.com/recommendations");
  sleep(1);
}
````

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure cache hit rate in your script?
- How would you adapt the test if external latency spikes?
- What thresholds would you set for p95 and error rate to pass a release gate?
